# Java quality checks (Spotless / Error Prone / SpotBugs)

Config: [`gradle/java-quality.gradle`](gradle/java-quality.gradle). JVM modules only (not Android AGP).

## When to run

| When                                | What                                                                             |
| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Day-to-day editing                  | Nothing — match **master** style by hand                                         |
| Agent / iterative `:core:test`      | Always `./gradlew :core:test -q -PerrorProneOff …` (skips EP warning flood)      |
| Format                              | `./gradlew spotlessApply` — same step as IDE Save (no ratchet)                   |
| Before PR / finishing a feature     | `./gradlew :core:spotlessCheck :core:test` (no `-PerrorProneOff`)                |
| Feature done / bug hunt (optional)  | `./gradlew :core:compileJava` (Error Prone warnings) and/or `:core:spotbugsMain` |
| Release / strict gate (opt-in only) | `-PerrorProneErrors` / `-PspotbugsErrors` — **not** default                      |

Do **not** run SpotBugs or Error Prone on every save, every small edit, or as a substitute for tests.
Agents must not run tests without `-q -PerrorProneOff` unless the user wants EP output or a PR gate.

## How to treat findings

- **Baseline is noisy** (especially outside `heroechoes/`). Do not mass-fix upstream SPD style hits (`PA_PUBLIC_*`, `ReferenceEquality` on Char/Item identity, public fields).
- Prefer fixing issues in **code you touched**, especially `heroechoes/` empty catches / unused code from Error Prone.
- SpotBugs HTML: `core/build/reports/spotbugs/main.html`. Error Prone: compile console only (no report folder).
- Spotless uses Eclipse JDT (`config/spotless/eclipse-java.xml` profile `iatdb`) with no ratchet. IDE Save and `./gradlew spotlessApply` run that same step (`-PspotlessIdeHook` on save).

## NullAway

NullAway runs as part of Error Prone, **scoped by package** — it only checks code inside
`AnnotatedPackages` (see [`gradle/java-quality.gradle`](gradle/java-quality.gradle)), so
turning it on does not light up the unannotated base-game sources. Currently scoped to
`heroechoes`, `effects`, and `actors` (with `actors.mobs` / `actors.hero` / `actors.buffs` /
`actors.blobs` excluded via `UnannotatedSubPackages`); widen one package at a time.

- Nullability is marked with the project-local
  [`com.watabou.utils.Nullable`](SPD-classes/src/main/java/com/watabou/utils/Nullable.java)
  (CLASS retention, so nothing reaches the Android or RoboVM runtime classpath).
- `AcknowledgeRestrictiveAnnotations=true` is required for NullAway to honour `@Nullable`
  on unannotated code at all. **It covers method parameters and return types, not fields** —
  a `@Nullable` field is only enforced once its own package is annotated. That is why
  `actors` is in the list: without it, `@Nullable` on
  [`Char.sprite`](core/src/main/java/com/shatteredpixel/shatteredpixeldungeon/actors/Char.java)
  is silently ignored and no sprite dereference is ever flagged.
- Severity follows the existing convention: `WARN` by default, `ERROR` under
  `-PerrorProneErrors`. Do not hard-code `-Xep:NullAway:ERROR`; an explicit severity
  overrides `allErrorsAsWarnings` and breaks the green-compile default.

## Prefer structural enforcement

For recurring bug classes, prefer making the compiler or test suite reject them
(encapsulation, NullAway/Error Prone scoped packages, source-scanning JUnit like
`RoboVMUnsupportedApisTest`) over patching call sites alone. Check whether a tool
supports **scoped** adoption before dismissing it as too invasive. Do not park useful
hardening in "deferred" — if it is good enough to describe in the plan, include it unless
the user cuts scope.

## Concurrent Gradle

Same as other long runs: `./gradlew --status` first; wait when BUSY for sequential work;
use a worktree only for planned parallel builds (see `gradle-worktree` rule).
